  "notes": "Glitchy multi-layered electropop producer/executive producer. The documented identity is one word and it is the whole drum block: \"the documented identity is glitch — stuttered, gated and micro-edited percussion layers over a four-square kick, with clap-and-snap stacks on 2&4 and sliced vocal fragments used as hats. Built in Ableton Live.\" So the kick is four-on-the-floor, the snare stack is clap-plus-snap at a tight offset, the perc lanes are `perc2`, `reverse` and `rim` at the widest density range in this batch (a stutter is a density spike, not a bed), and `hihat.base` stays 16th with a low fill density because the hats are vocal slices rather than a hat pattern — the roll vocabulary is off for the same reason. The velocity spread is deliberately wide, which is what micro-editing does to a level. The 808 is authored because the entry names it: \"filtered pumping synth sub on 8ths for the house mode; short 808 hits with glide for the trap-pop mode\", so `bass808` is `counter_riff` with a `staccato` sustain — short hits, not a sustained sub — while the pumping house sub stays as the bassline on `offbeat_8ths`. Harmony is \"minor-mode 4-bar loops with a bright relative-major chorus\", so a major family sits beside the minor ones, and \"he studied jazz guitar before producing, which shows in occasional extended voicings under otherwise plain dance harmony\" is why `seventh` and `ninth` carry more than a dance model normally would. \"Loops develop by adding processing rather than notes\" is the vamp weighting and the low melody density; \"countermelody is a chopped vocal or a plucked synth\" is `bell_echo` first. ⚠ `confidence` is medium against a medium-high rating; the entry's own [LOW-CONF] mark on the jazz-voicing claim is repeated here, and every drum probability, the humanize pair and `bpm.mode` 124 (the midpoint of the stated 120—128 house-facing band) are INFERRED.",
